    "https://www.bard.edu/humanresources/descriptions/?id=4860334": {
      "title": "The Harlem Chair for the Study of History and Culture",
      "description": "Job Opportunities at Bard College Skip to main content. Bard Bard Menu Academics sub-menu Academics Programs and Divisions Structure of the Curriculum Courses Requirements Academic Calendar College Catalogue Faculty Bard Abroad Bard NYC Dual-Degree Programs Bard Conservatory of Music Other Study Opportunities Graduate Programs Early Colleges Admission sub-menu Admission Applying Financial Aid Tuition + Payment Campus Tours Meet Our Students + Alumni/ae For Families / Familias Join Our Mailing List Contact Us Campus Life sub-menu Campus Life Living on Campus: Housing + Dining Campus Services + Resources Campus Activities New Students Visiting + Transportation Athletics + Recreation Montgomery Place Campus Civic Engagement sub-menu Civic Engagement Bard CCE Engaged Learning Student Leadership Grow Your Network About CCE Our Partners Get Involved Newsroom sub-menu News + Events Newsroom Events Calendar Press Releases Office of Communications Commencement Weekend Alumni/ae Reunion Family and Alumni/ae Weekend Fisher Center + SummerScape Athletic Events About Bard sub-menu About About Bard: Administration Bard History Campus Tours Mission Statement Love of Learning Visiting Bard Employment Support Bard Global Higher Education Alliance for the 21st Century Bard Abroad The Bard Network Inclusive Excellence Sustainability Title IX and Nondiscrimination Inside Bard Dean of the College Policy Library Giving Search Search Bard Employment Opportunities View All Opportunities HR Menu HR Menu Info sub-menu Information For: Applicants Current Employees New Employees Info sub-menu Bard Benefits Current Offerings TIAA Retirement Plan Emeritus Faculty Benefits Benefits Portal Info sub-menu Employee Resources Forms + Handbooks Training + Policies Wellness Affinity + Working Groups COVID-19 Workplace Rights Home Bard Prison Initiative/Microcollege The Harlem Chair for the Study of History and Culture Apply Here: https://apply.interfolio.com/195208 The Bard Prison Initiative (BPI) invites applications of distinguished scholars for an endowed, full-time faculty position, the Harlem Chair for the Study of History and Culture . This faculty member will be the professor-in-residence at the Bard Microcollege for Just Community Leadership, located in Harlem, New York. A Ph.D. in history, American studies, art history, anthropology, sociology, or a related field is expected. Strong candidates will have a combination of the following: a strong record of scholarly research and publication or artistic or literary production, a record of public facing intellectual work that is proximate to the Harlem community, and a record as distinguished teacher of diverse student populations in discussion-based seminars. This two-year position starts July 1, 2027 with teaching responsibilities beginning in the fall semester. Review of applications to begin January 8, 2027. The Chair will teach across the NYC Microcollege network and provide academic advising to students in Harlem. They will teach a 2-2 courseload, including writing intensive courses and one or more courses specifically focused on Harlem’s extraordinary past and present.  Additional responsibilities include organizing and participating in public lectures, collaborating with staff on student and student and community engagement, and creating connections between the Microcollege and collections of local libraries, cultural institutions and other humanistic resources. Candidates with connections to Harlem community and community institutions are particularly encouraged to apply. Successful candidates will be committed to innovative undergraduate teaching at a liberal arts college with a diverse student body; excel at working in a community that is broadly diverse with regard to race, ethnicity, socioeconomic status, gender, nationality, sexual orientation and religion; maintain an active research program; and be excited by rigorous, discussion-heavy and writing-based courses that center inclusive pedagogy and demand active participation of all students. Created in partnership with local institutions, the Bard Microcolleges serve communities often excluded from the university experience. Students enroll in full-time, in-person, seminar-style courses culminating in Bard College associate in arts degrees. Scholarships cover tuition and fees, and books are provided. The Bard Microcollege in Harlem is a partnership among JustLeadershipUSA, College & Community Fellowship, and the Bard Prison Initiative. A curriculum dedicated to advocacy, arts, and sciences provides the community leaders of tomorrow with a broad education that is classical and contemporary, artistic and practical, global and local. Consideration will be given to candidates who bring considerable experience and expertise to the position but who cannot commit to a two-year or full-time position. In this situation, salary is prorated and benefits eligibility depends on teaching load. About BPI: Formed in 1999 to address the mass incarceration crisis, the Bard Prison Initiative (BPI) defies expectations of who college is for and where it might lead. For over twenty years, BPI has reimagined and redefined questions of availability, affordability, and expectations typically associated with college in America. One of the most rigorous and effective college-in-prison programs in the country, BPI is now extending its radical intervention in educational inequity outside of prison through the Bard Microcolleges and BardBac. BPI currently enrolls nearly 600 students across eleven campuses. To Apply: To apply, please send a cover letter, curriculum vitae, teaching statement, and the names of three references who might speak to your teaching and collegiality to Interfolio by following this link: https://apply.interfolio.com/195208 . Compensation: $120,000 This salary range reflects the College's good faith and reasonable estimate of the compensation for the position at the time of the job posting. Salary decisions are dependent on several factors including but not limited to market and organizational considerations, experience, and qualifications of a selected candidate as well as internal and external equity. Benefits Overview: We are pleased to offer our full-time employees an excellent benefit package which includes multiple health insurance options; TIAA retirement plans; generous vacation, sick, and personal time; and more. To learn more about employee benefits and eligibility at Bard College, please visit https://www.bard.edu/humanresources/benefits/ . Equal Employment Opportunity Statement: Bard is an equal-opportunity employer, and we welcome applications from those who contribute to our diversity. All qualified candidates will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, sex, mental, or physical disability, age, sexual orientation, gender identity, national origin, familial status, veteran status, or genetic information. Bard is committed to providing access, equal opportunity, and reasonable accommodation for all individuals in employment practices, services, programs, and activities. AA/EOE Human Resources Quick Links ADP Workforce Now Payroll Website Office of Title IX and Nondiscrimination Dispute Resolution & Peace Building Office Office of the Dean of the College Contact Us Bard College Office of Human Resources Mailing Address: PO Box 5000, (Street Address: 30 Campus Road) Annandale-on-Hudson, NY 12504-5000 Phone: 845-758-7428 or Fax: 845-758-7826 Email: [email protected] We are open Monday through Friday, 9am - 5pm Equal Opportunity Employer Equal Opportunity Employer × Equal Opportunity Employer Bard College is an equal opportunity employer. All qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, sex, mental, or physical disability, age, sexual orientation, gender identity, national origin, familial status, veteran status, or genetic information. Bard is committed to providing access, equal opportunity, and reasonable accommodation for all individuals in employment practices, services, programs, and activities. 30 Campus Road, PO Box 5000 Annandale-on-Hudson, New York 12504-5000 Phone: 845-758-6822 Admission Email: [email protected] Bard has a long history of creating inclusive environments for all races, creeds, ethnicities, and genders. We will continue to monitor and adhere to all Federal and New York State laws and guidance. Information For Prospective Students Current Employees Alumni/ae Families ©2026 Bard College Quick Links Employment Travel to Bard Search Support Bard Bard IT Policies + Security Bard Privacy Notice"
    },
    "https://www.bard.edu/humanresources/descriptions/?id=5932940": {
      "title": "Visiting Faculty Position: Career Development",
      "description": "Job Opportunities at Bard College Skip to main content. Bard Bard Menu Academics sub-menu Academics Programs and Divisions Structure of the Curriculum Courses Requirements Academic Calendar College Catalogue Faculty Bard Abroad Bard NYC Dual-Degree Programs Bard Conservatory of Music Other Study Opportunities Graduate Programs Early Colleges Admission sub-menu Admission Applying Financial Aid Tuition + Payment Campus Tours Meet Our Students + Alumni/ae For Families / Familias Join Our Mailing List Contact Us Campus Life sub-menu Campus Life Living on Campus: Housing + Dining Campus Services + Resources Campus Activities New Students Visiting + Transportation Athletics + Recreation Montgomery Place Campus Civic Engagement sub-menu Civic Engagement Bard CCE Engaged Learning Student Leadership Grow Your Network About CCE Our Partners Get Involved Newsroom sub-menu News + Events Newsroom Events Calendar Press Releases Office of Communications Commencement Weekend Alumni/ae Reunion Family and Alumni/ae Weekend Fisher Center + SummerScape Athletic Events About Bard sub-menu About About Bard: Administration Bard History Campus Tours Mission Statement Love of Learning Visiting Bard Employment Support Bard Global Higher Education Alliance for the 21st Century Bard Abroad The Bard Network Inclusive Excellence Sustainability Title IX and Nondiscrimination Inside Bard Dean of the College Policy Library Giving Search Search Bard Employment Opportunities View All Opportunities HR Menu HR Menu Info sub-menu Information For: Applicants Current Employees New Employees Info sub-menu Bard Benefits Current Offerings TIAA Retirement Plan Emeritus Faculty Benefits Benefits Portal Info sub-menu Employee Resources Forms + Handbooks Training + Policies Wellness Affinity + Working Groups COVID-19 Workplace Rights Home MBA in Sustainability Visiting Faculty Position: Career Development Apply Here: https://apply.interfolio.com/191202 Bard College’s low-residency MBA in Sustainability program, based in New York City, has an opening for a part-time, visiting faculty member to lead a non-credit-bearing advisory in Career Development.  Bard’s MBA program fully integrates sustainability into a business curriculum, with students mastering the business case for sustainability, understanding how to align profit with social and environmental goals. In addition, all courses cultivate the development of business leaders committed to Justice in Organizations and Impacts. This position is for a Faculty Career Advisor.  Working with the MBA Director, the Advisor meets one-on-one with each of the students in the program every semester to develop an Individual Career Plan. The Advisor also teaches a monthly workshop on career development strategy, and consults as needed with individual students on career needs. The Advisor serves on the joint student, faculty and alumni Careers Committee. The workload for this position is equivalent to two, 2-credit courses per year. This requires 6-7 hours per week of work during the spring and fall semesters, and 2 hours per week during the summer. The position is largely remote, with occasional in-person meetings. Candidates should live within commuting distance of NYC. Qualifications: MBA faculty members are expected to have earned a Ph.D., J.D., M.B.A., M.A., or equivalent degree. Successful candidates will have an established record of excellence in teaching.  For more information about Bard’s MBA program, visit the MBA website: https://www.bard.edu/mba . To Apply: Please provide a cover letter, resume, and the contact information for three professional references to Interfolio by following this link: https://apply.interfolio.com/191202 . In your cover letter, in addition to explaining your work in career development, please address how your advising would contribute to supporting a diverse student body with an equitable and inclusive approach. Applications will be reviewed as received. Compensation: $18,000 for the year This salary range reflects the College's good faith and reasonable estimate of the compensation for the position at the time of the job posting. Salary decisions are dependent on several factors including but not limited to market and organizational considerations, experience, and qualifications of a selected candidate as well as internal and external equity. Benefits Overview: We are pleased to offer our full-time employees an excellent benefit package which includes multiple health insurance options; TIAA retirement plans; generous vacation, sick, and personal time; and more. To learn more about employee benefits and eligibility at Bard College, please visit https://www.bard.edu/humanresources/benefits/ . Equal Employment Opportunity Statement: Bard is an equal-opportunity employer, and we welcome applications from those who contribute to our diversity. All qualified candidates will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, sex, mental, or physical disability, age, sexual orientation, gender identity, national origin, familial status, veteran status, or genetic information. Bard is committed to providing access, equal opportunity, and reasonable accommodation for all individuals in employment practices, services, programs, and activities. AA/EOE Human Resources Quick Links ADP Workforce Now Payroll Website Office of Title IX and Nondiscrimination Dispute Resolution & Peace Building Office Office of the Dean of the College Contact Us Bard College Office of Human Resources Mailing Address: PO Box 5000, (Street Address: 30 Campus Road) Annandale-on-Hudson, NY 12504-5000 Phone: 845-758-7428 or Fax: 845-758-7826 Email: [email protected] We are open Monday through Friday, 9am - 5pm Equal Opportunity Employer Equal Opportunity Employer × Equal Opportunity Employer Bard College is an equal opportunity employer. All qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, sex, mental, or physical disability, age, sexual orientation, gender identity, national origin, familial status, veteran status, or genetic information. Bard is committed to providing access, equal opportunity, and reasonable accommodation for all individuals in employment practices, services, programs, and activities. 30 Campus Road, PO Box 5000 Annandale-on-Hudson, New York 12504-5000 Phone: 845-758-6822 Admission Email: [email protected] Bard has a long history of creating inclusive environments for all races, creeds, ethnicities, and genders. We will continue to monitor and adhere to all Federal and New York State laws and guidance. Information For Prospective Students Current Employees Alumni/ae Families ©2026 Bard College Quick Links Employment Travel to Bard Search Support Bard Bard IT Policies + Security Bard Privacy Notice"
