    "https://www.bard.edu/humanresources/descriptions/?id=1801925": {
      "title": "The BPI Endowed Chair for the Study of History",
      "description": "Job Opportunities at Bard College Skip to main content. Bard Bard Menu Academics sub-menu Academics Programs and Divisions Structure of the Curriculum Courses Requirements Academic Calendar College Catalogue Faculty Bard Abroad Bard NYC Dual-Degree Programs Bard Conservatory of Music Other Study Opportunities Graduate Programs Early Colleges Admission sub-menu Admission Applying Financial Aid Tuition + Payment Campus Tours Meet Our Students + Alumni/ae For Families / Familias Join Our Mailing List Contact Us Campus Life sub-menu Campus Life Living on Campus: Housing + Dining Campus Services + Resources Campus Activities New Students Visiting + Transportation Athletics + Recreation Montgomery Place Campus Civic Engagement sub-menu Civic Engagement Bard CCE Engaged Learning Student Leadership Grow Your Network About CCE Our Partners Get Involved Newsroom sub-menu News + Events Newsroom Events Calendar Press Releases Office of Communications Commencement Weekend Alumni/ae Reunion Family and Alumni/ae Weekend Fisher Center + SummerScape Athletic Events About Bard sub-menu About About Bard: Administration Bard History Campus Tours Mission Statement Love of Learning Visiting Bard Employment Support Bard Global Higher Education Alliance for the 21st Century Bard Abroad The Bard Network Inclusive Excellence Sustainability Title IX and Nondiscrimination Inside Bard Dean of the College Policy Library Giving Search Search Bard Employment Opportunities View All Opportunities HR Menu HR Menu Info sub-menu Information For: Applicants Current Employees New Employees Info sub-menu Bard Benefits Current Offerings TIAA Retirement Plan Emeritus Faculty Benefits Benefits Portal Info sub-menu Employee Resources Forms + Handbooks Training + Policies Wellness Affinity + Working Groups COVID-19 Workplace Rights Home Bard Prison Initiative The BPI Endowed Chair for the Study of History Apply Here: https://apply.interfolio.com/196239 The Bard Prison Initiative (BPI) invites nominations and applications of distinguished scholars for the Chair for the Study of History . A Ph.D. in history or related field is required; research specialization is open. This two-year full-time position starts July 1, 2027 with teaching responsibilities beginning in the fall semester. Review of applications to begin January 8, 2027. Successful candidates will be committed to innovative undergraduate teaching at a liberal arts college with a diverse student body; excel at working in a community that is broadly diverse with regard to race, ethnicity, socioeconomic status, gender, nationality, sexual orientation and religion; maintain an active research program; and be excited by rigorous, discussion-heavy and writing-based courses that center inclusive pedagogy and demand active, ambitious participation of all students. The Chair will teach a 2-2 course load across six of BPI’s seven campuses located in correctional facilities in New York State. Classes will contribute to the academic depth and breadth of the undergraduate and/or graduate curriculum through courses in the Chair’s field of study, including writing intensive courses. Additional participation in BPI faculty workshops, guest lectures, senior project advising and overall engagement in BPI is expected. BPI offers associate’s degrees in liberal studies; bachelor’s degrees in social studies, literature and humanities, and mathematics along with specializations in public health, education, and biology; and master’s degrees in public humanities. Faculty must be able to provide their own transportation to prisons across the Hudson Valley, and they must successfully complete the Department of Corrections process for approved entry into the prisons. Please note that the prison is a location of BPI coursework, not the subject matter of courses, which reflect the breadth of liberal arts education on the main campus of Bard College. This position does not include an opportunity to conduct research within the prisons or to approach students as research subjects. Consideration will be given to candidates who bring considerable experience and expertise to the position but who cannot commit to a two-year or full-time position. In this situation, salary is prorated and benefits eligibility depends on teaching load. About BPI: Formed in 1999 to address the mass incarceration crisis, the Bard Prison Initiative (BPI) defies expectations of who college is for and where it might lead. For over twenty years, BPI has reimagined and redefined questions of availability, affordability, and expectations typically associated with college in America. One of the most rigorous and effective college-in-prison programs in the country, BPI is now extending its radical intervention in educational inequity outside of prison through the Bard Microcolleges and BardBac. BPI currently enrolls nearly 600 students across eleven campuses. To Apply: To apply, please send a cover letter, curriculum vitae, teaching statement, and the names of three references who might speak to your teaching and collegiality to Interfolio by following this link: https://apply.interfolio.com/196239 . Compensation: $120,000 This salary range reflects the College's good faith and reasonable estimate of the compensation for the position at the time of the job posting. Salary decisions are dependent on several factors including but not limited to market and organizational considerations, experience, and qualifications of a selected candidate as well as internal and external equity. Benefits Overview: We are pleased to offer our full-time employees an excellent benefit package which includes multiple health insurance options; TIAA retirement plans; generous vacation, sick, and personal time; and more. To learn more about employee benefits and eligibility at Bard College, please visit https://www.bard.edu/humanresources/benefits/ . Equal Employment Opportunity Statement: Bard is an equal-opportunity employer, and we welcome applications from those who contribute to our diversity. All qualified candidates will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, sex, mental, or physical disability, age, sexual orientation, gender identity, national origin, familial status, veteran status, or genetic information. Bard is committed to providing access, equal opportunity, and reasonable accommodation for all individuals in employment practices, services, programs, and activities. AA/EOE Human Resources Quick Links ADP Workforce Now Payroll Website Office of Title IX and Nondiscrimination Dispute Resolution & Peace Building Office Office of the Dean of the College Contact Us Bard College Office of Human Resources Mailing Address: PO Box 5000, (Street Address: 30 Campus Road) Annandale-on-Hudson, NY 12504-5000 Phone: 845-758-7428 or Fax: 845-758-7826 Email: [email protected] We are open Monday through Friday, 9am - 5pm Equal Opportunity Employer Equal Opportunity Employer × Equal Opportunity Employer Bard College is an equal opportunity employer. All qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, sex, mental, or physical disability, age, sexual orientation, gender identity, national origin, familial status, veteran status, or genetic information. Bard is committed to providing access, equal opportunity, and reasonable accommodation for all individuals in employment practices, services, programs, and activities. 30 Campus Road, PO Box 5000 Annandale-on-Hudson, New York 12504-5000 Phone: 845-758-6822 Admission Email: [email protected] Bard has a long history of creating inclusive environments for all races, creeds, ethnicities, and genders. We will continue to monitor and adhere to all Federal and New York State laws and guidance. Information For Prospective Students Current Employees Alumni/ae Families ©2026 Bard College Quick Links Employment Travel to Bard Search Support Bard Bard IT Policies + Security Bard Privacy Notice"
    },
    "https://www.bard.edu/humanresources/descriptions/?id=5166723": {
      "title": "The 2027 Anthony Lester Fellowships",
      "description": "Job Opportunities at Bard College Skip to main content. Bard Bard Menu Academics sub-menu Academics Programs and Divisions Structure of the Curriculum Courses Requirements Academic Calendar College Catalogue Faculty Bard Abroad Bard NYC Dual-Degree Programs Bard Conservatory of Music Other Study Opportunities Graduate Programs Early Colleges Admission sub-menu Admission Applying Financial Aid Tuition + Payment Campus Tours Meet Our Students + Alumni/ae For Families / Familias Join Our Mailing List Contact Us Campus Life sub-menu Campus Life Living on Campus: Housing + Dining Campus Services + Resources Campus Activities New Students Visiting + Transportation Athletics + Recreation Montgomery Place Campus Civic Engagement sub-menu Civic Engagement Bard CCE Engaged Learning Student Leadership Grow Your Network About CCE Our Partners Get Involved Newsroom sub-menu News + Events Newsroom Events Calendar Press Releases Office of Communications Commencement Weekend Alumni/ae Reunion Family and Alumni/ae Weekend Fisher Center + SummerScape Athletic Events About Bard sub-menu About About Bard: Administration Bard History Campus Tours Mission Statement Love of Learning Visiting Bard Employment Support Bard Global Higher Education Alliance for the 21st Century Bard Abroad The Bard Network Inclusive Excellence Sustainability Title IX and Nondiscrimination Inside Bard Dean of the College Policy Library Giving Search Search Bard Employment Opportunities View All Opportunities HR Menu HR Menu Info sub-menu Information For: Applicants Current Employees New Employees Info sub-menu Bard Benefits Current Offerings TIAA Retirement Plan Emeritus Faculty Benefits Benefits Portal Info sub-menu Employee Resources Forms + Handbooks Training + Policies Wellness Affinity + Working Groups COVID-19 Workplace Rights Home Human Rights Project The 2027 Anthony Lester Fellowships Apply Here: https://apply.interfolio.com/196282 The Anthony Lester Fellowships support lawyers or law students undertaking practical fieldwork in human rights and the rule of law. The fellowships honour the memory and legacy of Anthony Lester QC (Lord Lester of Herne Hill), one of Britain’s most distinguished human rights lawyers. As a young lawyer, he twice visited the American South during the civil rights movement and wrote a report on race relations. His experiences there inspired his pioneering work as a barrister and legislator. The Anthony Lester Fellowships are intended to support lawyers and law students early in their careers to undertake a project of their choosing that helps affect positive, practical change in relation to the rule of law and human rights, inspired by his experience. The fellowships are designed to encourage younger advocates, who may not otherwise have the opportunity, to undertake practical fieldwork overseas, for a minimum period of 3 months. Projects should take place in a part of the world where applicants do not live or work, and which is substantially new to them. The fellowships are administered by the Human Rights Project at Bard College, and endowed by the Gatsby Charitable Foundation, the Sigrid Rausing Trust, and the Lester family. How to Apply: The fellowships are open to lawyers or law students early in their careers. Applicants should submit the following by February 19, 2027: A cover letter, briefly summarising the applicant’s background and proposed project (on one page) A proposal (1250 words maximum) for their chosen project A curriculum vitae Contact information for two references Please note that AI may not be used in the generation of any of these materials. Applicants will be informed of the results of the selection process by mid May 2027. All interested applicants can apply at the following link: https://apply.interfolio.com/196282 . Terms of the Fellowship: Two fellowships will be awarded for activities beginning no sooner than July 2027. Fellows will be awarded a stipend of US $25,000 to cover their costs during the fellowship. It is expected that the project will comprise full-time work for the fellow for a minimum of three months.  At the end of the fellowship, fellows will be invited to give a presentation at Bard’s campus in New York’s Hudson Valley (their travel and accommodation costs will be paid). Fellows will write a short report on their project, to be published on the programme’s website. Fellows may wish to publish their report elsewhere (crediting their support from the Anthony Lester Fellowships at Bard College). They will also have the chance to discuss their work with those who worked with Anthony Lester at the Odysseus Trust, which supported his legislative work. About Anthony Lester: Anthony Lester (1936-2020) was an eminent human rights barrister and legislator. He argued leading cases in British, European and Commonwealth courts and developed the burgeoning field of public law. He campaigned for thirty years for the Human Rights Act 1998. As special adviser to Roy Jenkins, he helped devise the Sex Discrimination and Race Relations Acts. His activities in the House of Lords led to legislation on civil partnership, forced marriage, equality and defamation reform. He worked with human rights NGOs at home and overseas and founded the human rights charity Interights. The ideas that motivated him are recorded in his book Five Ideas to Fight For (2016). About Bard College: Founded in 1860, Bard College is a four-year residential college of the liberal arts and sciences located 90 miles north of New York City. With the addition of the Montgomery Place estate, Bard’s campus consists of nearly 1,000 park-like acres in the Hudson River Valley. It offers bachelor of arts, bachelor of science, and bachelor of music degrees, with majors in nearly 40 academic programs, graduate degrees in 13 programs, eight early colleges, and numerous dual-degree programs nationally and internationally. Building on its 163-year history as a competitive and innovative undergraduate institution, Bard College has expanded its mission as a private institution acting in the public interest across the country and around the world to meet broader student needs and increase access to liberal education. The undergraduate program at our main campus in upstate New York has a reputation for scholarly excellence, a focus on the arts, and civic engagement. Bard is committed to enriching culture, public life, and democratic discourse by training tomorrow’s thought leaders. For more information about Bard College, visit https://bard.edu . Equal Employment Opportunity Statement: Bard is an equal-opportunity employer, and we welcome applications from those who contribute to our diversity. All qualified candidates will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, sex, mental, or physical disability, age, sexual orientation, gender identity, national origin, familial status, veteran status, or genetic information. Bard is committed to providing access, equal opportunity, and reasonable accommodation for all individuals in employment practices, services, programs, and activities. AA/EOE Human Resources Quick Links ADP Workforce Now Payroll Website Office of Title IX and Nondiscrimination Dispute Resolution & Peace Building Office Office of the Dean of the College Contact Us Bard College Office of Human Resources Mailing Address: PO Box 5000, (Street Address: 30 Campus Road) Annandale-on-Hudson, NY 12504-5000 Phone: 845-758-7428 or Fax: 845-758-7826 Email: hr@bard.edu We are open Monday through Friday, 9am - 5pm Equal Opportunity Employer Equal Opportunity Employer 30 Campus Road, PO Box 5000 Annandale-on-Hudson, New York 12504-5000 Phone: 845-758-6822 Admission Email: admission@bard.edu Bard has a long history of creating inclusive environments for all races, creeds, ethnicities, and genders. We will continue to monitor and adhere to all Federal and New York State laws and guidance. Information For Prospective Students Current Employees Alumni/ae Families ©2026 Bard College Quick Links Employment Travel to Bard Search Support Bard Bard IT Policies + Security Bard Privacy Notice × Equal Opportunity Employer Bard College is an equal opportunity employer. All qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, sex, mental, or physical disability, age, sexual orientation, gender identity, national origin, familial status, veteran status, or genetic information. Bard is committed to providing access, equal opportunity, and reasonable accommodation for all individuals in employment practices, services, programs, and activities."
    },
    "https://www.bard.edu/humanresources/descriptions/?id=1068848": {
      "title": "Counselor/Therapist",
      "description": "Job Opportunities at Bard College Skip to main content. Bard Bard Menu Academics sub-menu Academics Programs and Divisions Structure of the Curriculum Courses Requirements Academic Calendar College Catalogue Faculty Bard Abroad Bard NYC Dual-Degree Programs Bard Conservatory of Music Other Study Opportunities Graduate Programs Early Colleges Admission sub-menu Admission Applying Financial Aid Tuition + Payment Campus Tours Meet Our Students + Alumni/ae For Families / Familias Join Our Mailing List Contact Us Campus Life sub-menu Campus Life Living on Campus: Housing + Dining Campus Services + Resources Campus Activities New Students Visiting + Transportation Athletics + Recreation Montgomery Place Campus Civic Engagement sub-menu Civic Engagement Bard CCE Engaged Learning Student Leadership Grow Your Network About CCE Our Partners Get Involved Newsroom sub-menu News + Events Newsroom Events Calendar Press Releases Office of Communications Commencement Weekend Alumni/ae Reunion Family and Alumni/ae Weekend Fisher Center + SummerScape Athletic Events About Bard sub-menu About About Bard: Administration Bard History Campus Tours Mission Statement Love of Learning Visiting Bard Employment Support Bard Global Higher Education Alliance for the 21st Century Bard Abroad The Bard Network Inclusive Excellence Sustainability Title IX and Nondiscrimination Inside Bard Dean of the College Policy Library Giving Search Search Bard Employment Opportunities View All Opportunities HR Menu HR Menu Info sub-menu Information For: Applicants Current Employees New Employees Info sub-menu Bard Benefits Current Offerings TIAA Retirement Plan Emeritus Faculty Benefits Benefits Portal Info sub-menu Employee Resources Forms + Handbooks Training + Policies Wellness Affinity + Working Groups COVID-19 Workplace Rights Home Bard College Counselor/Therapist Apply Here: https://apply.interfolio.com/196399 Bard College Counseling Services is hiring a full-time Mental Health Counselor who has experience working with college students. The counselor will work collaboratively with Counseling Services staff and campus partners to promote student well being, academic success, and personal development. This permanent position works four days per week for 10 months per year. The position’s hours are 9:00am-5:00 pm. About Bard Counseling Services: Our multidisciplinary staff consists of psychologists, clinical social workers, mental health counselors, graduate interns, and a psychiatrist. Our mission is to provide high quality mental health services, referrals, and educational programming to Bard students. We are committed to inclusive excellence and the promotion of social justice. We appreciate and honor the rich diversity of the institution and work with all students with compassion, respect, and dignity. About Bard: Bard College’s main campus is in Annandale-on-Hudson, N.Y., with 1,900 undergraduate students; the College also includes multiple graduate programs. The 1,000-acre campus sits along the Hudson River and offers the Fisher Performing Arts Center, and Hessel Museum of Art, and is the home of the Bard Prison Initiative, the largest college-degree-granting prison education program in the U.S. The Bard Network also includes several Bard High School Early Colleges across the country as well as international campuses and partnerships. Bard College is a private institution working in the public interest. Bard faculty and staff lead their students by example, building upon the existing network of boundary-breaking programs focused on rethinking who can and should be included in a liberal arts education. Essential Duties and Responsibilities: Provide individual, group, and couples counseling to students presenting with a variety of personal, social, academic, and mental health concerns Provide crisis assessment and intervention during work hours and on call rotation for evenings and weekends Consult and collaborate with campus partners, including participation in campus-wide committees Develop and deliver creative and innovative programming, including groups, workshops, and events designed specifically to college students Serve as the primary coordinator of the Counseling Center’s group counseling program, overseeing the development, implementation, coordination, and evaluation of ongoing and specialized student groups Provide direct group therapy and facilitate groups addressing a range of student concerns Other duties as assigned Qualifications: License to practice counseling independently in New York State or If from out of state, New York licensed-eligible within one year after hire (LCAT, LCSW, LMHC, Licensed Psychologist) Interest and experience presenting trainings and outreach programs to students, faculty, and staff Experience providing group therapy Experience providing counseling to college students Outstanding interpersonal skills and ability to work effectively and cooperatively with a team of professionals Ability to maintain up to date and accurate documentation To Apply: Please provide a cover letter, resume, and the contact information for three professional references to Interfolio by following this link: https://apply.interfolio.com/196399 . Compensation: $62,500 to $65,000 annually This salary range reflects the College's good faith and reasonable estimate of the compensation for the position at the time of the job posting. Salary decisions are dependent on several factors including but not limited to market and organizational considerations, experience, and qualifications of a selected candidate as well as internal and external equity. Benefits Overview: We are pleased to offer our full-time employees an excellent benefit package which includes multiple health insurance options; TIAA retirement plans; generous vacation, sick, and personal time; and more. To learn more about employee benefits and eligibility at Bard College, please visit https://www.bard.edu/humanresources/benefits/ . Equal Employment Opportunity Statement: Bard is an equal-opportunity employer, and we welcome applications from those who contribute to our diversity. All qualified candidates will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, sex, mental, or physical disability, age, sexual orientation, gender identity, national origin, familial status, veteran status, or genetic information. Bard is committed to providing access, equal opportunity, and reasonable accommodation for all individuals in employment practices, services, programs, and activities. AA/EOE Human Resources Quick Links ADP Workforce Now Payroll Website Office of Title IX and Nondiscrimination Dispute Resolution & Peace Building Office Office of the Dean of the College Contact Us Bard College Office of Human Resources Mailing Address: PO Box 5000, (Street Address: 30 Campus Road) Annandale-on-Hudson, NY 12504-5000 Phone: 845-758-7428 or Fax: 845-758-7826 Email: hr@bard.edu We are open Monday through Friday, 9am - 5pm Equal Opportunity Employer Equal Opportunity Employer 30 Campus Road, PO Box 5000 Annandale-on-Hudson, New York 12504-5000 Phone: 845-758-6822 Admission Email: admission@bard.edu Bard has a long history of creating inclusive environments for all races, creeds, ethnicities, and genders. We will continue to monitor and adhere to all Federal and New York State laws and guidance. Information For Prospective Students Current Employees Alumni/ae Families ©2026 Bard College Quick Links Employment Travel to Bard Search Support Bard Bard IT Policies + Security Bard Privacy Notice × Equal Opportunity Employer Bard College is an equal opportunity employer. All qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, sex, mental, or physical disability, age, sexual orientation, gender identity, national origin, familial status, veteran status, or genetic information. Bard is committed to providing access, equal opportunity, and reasonable accommodation for all individuals in employment practices, services, programs, and activities."
    },
    "https://www.bard.edu/humanresources/descriptions/?id=5455332": {
      "title": "Procurement Secretary",
      "description": "Job Opportunities at Bard College Skip to main content. Bard Bard Menu Academics sub-menu Academics Programs and Divisions Structure of the Curriculum Courses Requirements Academic Calendar College Catalogue Faculty Bard Abroad Bard NYC Dual-Degree Programs Bard Conservatory of Music Other Study Opportunities Graduate Programs Early Colleges Admission sub-menu Admission Applying Financial Aid Tuition + Payment Campus Tours Meet Our Students + Alumni/ae For Families / Familias Join Our Mailing List Contact Us Campus Life sub-menu Campus Life Living on Campus: Housing + Dining Campus Services + Resources Campus Activities New Students Visiting + Transportation Athletics + Recreation Montgomery Place Campus Civic Engagement sub-menu Civic Engagement Bard CCE Engaged Learning Student Leadership Grow Your Network About CCE Our Partners Get Involved Newsroom sub-menu News + Events Newsroom Events Calendar Press Releases Office of Communications Commencement Weekend Alumni/ae Reunion Family and Alumni/ae Weekend Fisher Center + SummerScape Athletic Events About Bard sub-menu About About Bard: Administration Bard History Campus Tours Mission Statement Love of Learning Visiting Bard Employment Support Bard Global Higher Education Alliance for the 21st Century Bard Abroad The Bard Network Inclusive Excellence Sustainability Title IX and Nondiscrimination Inside Bard Dean of the College Policy Library Giving Search Search Bard Employment Opportunities View All Opportunities HR Menu HR Menu Info sub-menu Information For: Applicants Current Employees New Employees Info sub-menu Bard Benefits Current Offerings TIAA Retirement Plan Emeritus Faculty Benefits Benefits Portal Info sub-menu Employee Resources Forms + Handbooks Training + Policies Wellness Affinity + Working Groups COVID-19 Workplace Rights Home Bard High School Early College Bronx Procurement Secretary Apply Here: https://apply.interfolio.com/196285 About the School Bard High School Early College Bronx (BHSEC Bronx) is a NYC Department of Education public school operated in partnership with Bard College. Students earn both a New York State Regents diploma and an Associate in Arts degree from Bard College, tuition-free. We seek an organized, detail-oriented Procurement Secretary to manage the school's purchasing, vendor relations, and related fiscal records. Position Summary The Procurement Secretary oversees the full purchasing cycle for the school, from requisition through payment and reconciliation, ensuring that all purchases comply with DOE procurement policies. The position reports to the Principal and works closely with the Assistant Principals, department leaders, the payroll secretary, and the Bard College partnership office. Key Responsibilities Purchasing and Procurement Process purchase requisitions and orders in FAMIS and SHOPDOE, using approved DOE contract vendors where available. Obtain and document competitive quotes and bids in accordance with DOE small-purchase thresholds and procedures. Code purchases accurately to the correct funding sources and object codes in coordination with the school's Galaxy budget. Track open orders, follow up with vendors on delivery, and resolve discrepancies, back-orders, and returns. Record receipt of goods and services in FAMIS to ensure timely vendor payment. Procurement Card and Reimbursements Administer the school's credit card, maintain receipts, and complete monthly reconciliations. Process staff reimbursements and travel-related paperwork in accordance with DOE and Bard College policy. Vendor and Contract Management Assist new vendors with DOE vendor registration and maintain an up-to-date vendor file. Prepare and track paperwork for consultant and service contracts, including programs and field trips. Budget Support and Recordkeeping Monitor spending against budget allocations and provide regular expenditure reports to the Principal. Support NYSTL (textbook, library, software, and hardware) ordering and tracking. Maintain the school's equipment inventory and asset records. Assist with Student Activity Fund documentation and imprest/petty cash records as assigned. Maintain organized, audit-ready files for all procurement transactions. General Office Support Serve as the point of contact for staff purchasing requests and explain procedures clearly. Provide general administrative support to the main office as needed. Location 1619 Boston Rd, Bronx, NY 10460 Salary and Benefits The salary is based on the UFT school secretary salary schedule and depends on education, certificates and experience. The position includes NYC DOE benefits, including health insurance, pension, and paid leave. Start Date: November 2026 Qualifications Requirements Associate's or bachelor's degree preferred. Experience in purchasing, accounts payable, or administrative office work. Strong proficiency in Microsoft Excel and Google Workspace. Excellent organizational skills and attention to detail, with the ability to manage multiple deadlines. Ability to handle confidential financial information with discretion. Clear written and verbal communication skills. Preferred Qualifications Experience with NYC DOE systems, including FAMIS, SHOPDOE, and Galaxy. Familiarity with the DOE procurement policies. Prior experience in a school or public-sector setting. Bilingual ability (Spanish or other languages spoken in the school community) is a plus. Application Instructions To apply, please submit a current resume or curriculum vitae and a brief cover letter describing your interest in the position and relevant experience through the Interfolio application portal link for this position (you will need to find the \"Apply Now\" button found on the upper right corner on the Interfolio page): https://apply.interfolio.com/196285 Applications will be reviewed until the position is filled. Questions Inquiries may be directed via email with the subject line “Procurement Secretary” to Siska Brutsaert, Principal, at sbrutsaert@bhsec.bard.edu AA/EOE Human Resources Quick Links ADP Workforce Now Payroll Website Office of Title IX and Nondiscrimination Dispute Resolution & Peace Building Office Office of the Dean of the College Contact Us Bard College Office of Human Resources Mailing Address: PO Box 5000, (Street Address: 30 Campus Road) Annandale-on-Hudson, NY 12504-5000 Phone: 845-758-7428 or Fax: 845-758-7826 Email: hr@bard.edu We are open Monday through Friday, 9am - 5pm Equal Opportunity Employer Equal Opportunity Employer 30 Campus Road, PO Box 5000 Annandale-on-Hudson, New York 12504-5000 Phone: 845-758-6822 Admission Email: admission@bard.edu Bard has a long history of creating inclusive environments for all races, creeds, ethnicities, and genders. We will continue to monitor and adhere to all Federal and New York State laws and guidance. Information For Prospective Students Current Employees Alumni/ae Families ©2026 Bard College Quick Links Employment Travel to Bard Search Support Bard Bard IT Policies + Security Bard Privacy Notice × Equal Opportunity Employer Bard College is an equal opportunity employer. All qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, sex, mental, or physical disability, age, sexual orientation, gender identity, national origin, familial status, veteran status, or genetic information. Bard is committed to providing access, equal opportunity, and reasonable accommodation for all individuals in employment practices, services, programs, and activities."
    }
